</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, August 19, 2005 - 11:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rader is simple. <BR> <BR>He simply never learned to deal with the original sin: &#34;self focus&#34;. <BR> <BR>Everyone is born with a certain amount of self focus. That is what makes us breath and eat. Most people learn, however, that there is a limits to self-gratification.  <BR> <BR>Rader never got it.  <BR> <BR>A lot of people don&#39;t get it. The reason Rader became such a festering sore of a personality, though, was his ability to uber-compartmentalize his personality so that he could live a life of hypocracy, keeping the personality that he KNEW was unacceptable hidden, and exposing the personality that he KNEW was acceptable. <BR> <BR>Preventing more Raders? <BR> <BR>I see four common trends today that are contributing to the Raderesque behaviour: <BR> <BR>1&#41; acceptance of narcissism as a healthy self image. Examples: artificially inflating child&#39;s &#34;self-esteem&#34;, &#34;Army of One&#34; &#40;illusion used as an enlistment ploy that is, of course, corrected during boot camp&#41;, and Donald Trump &#40;and reality TV in general&#41;. <BR> <BR>2&#41; Acceptance of Compartmentalized behavior and hypocracy as normal. Examples: Rush Limbaugh&#39;s insistance that drug users are criminal instead of in need of medical attention...until he went into to rehab for oxycotin, Catholic Church harboring peodophile priests from prosecution, and politics...in general. <BR> <BR>3&#41; The &#34;extreme thing&#34;. The Ancient Greeks &#40;who were wise&#41; stressed &#34;moderation in all things&#34;. Our modern society puts a premium on &#34;Xtreme&#34;. Extreme sports, colors, flavors, fashions, etc.. Although extremes are more exciting, if you do everything to the nth extreme, you are going to run into problems. <BR> <BR>4&#41; The ever-popular desensetizing of the young to violence through video games and gross movies. I&#39;m not real sure about this one, but I don&#39;t remember any mass murders at schools before video games and cable. <BR> <BR>My basic point is: Take a kid, inflate his ego, teach him that hypocracy is okay and extreme behavior is good, then desensetize him to violence, and you will end up with someone like Rader. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 14071-->
<!--Post: 14072--><!--Time: 1124523316-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST14072"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, August 20, 2005 - 12:35 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->What you point out may well be contributing factors to a future Dennis Rader however no one told Rader that hypocracy was OK and there is no indication in his family of being taught extreme behavior and a desensetization to violence. <BR>There was no Rush Limbaughs or Donald Trumps, no reality shows, no extreme sports or extreme anything. He grew up in the Eisenhower 50&#39;s. There wasn&#39;t too many gross movies and there wasn&#39;t video or cable and he wasn&#39;t exposed to any Catholic church pedophelic priests. <BR>He had a good family, lived in a good area, went to good schools, had disiplined miltary training and lasted through his tour of duty and wasn&#39;t in combat. He began a college education and married into a good family.  <BR>And yet he became what he became, when came 1974 and the darkness of darkness spewed forth, springing from him like a volcanic burst. <BR>And he became what he became. <BR>And it was there all the while. But with no criminal background or history of observed phychiatric problems and his failure to disclose that anything was wrong with him how can you prevent that?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
